reliability:BS-17 a reliability:BlindSpot,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BS-17: Forward-Only Verification Bias"@en ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "NEEDS_EXPERT_REVIEW: a reliability ontology-native concept. Related to bias blind spot (Pronin, Lin, Ross, 2002. JPSP)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition """Blind spot in which an assessor constructs verification exclusively 
    from the confirmation direction — testing that desired properties ARE 
    present — without ever testing that undesired properties are ABSENT. 
    This bias is particularly dangerous for completeness claims because 
    forward verification has zero sensitivity to omissions: checking 
    'does Turkish exist for item X?' tells you nothing about items Y and Z 
    that were never checked.
    
    The bias is self-reinforcing through NarrativeAnchoring: high forward-
    audit scores (210/210) create a completeness narrative that suppresses 
    the impulse to check from the other direction. Each version's perfect 
    score anchors the next version's verification design.
    
    Distinguished from BS-14/BS-15 (verification adequacy blind spots) by 
    specificity: those address the general principle of checking whether 
    verification CAN detect the failure; BS-17 addresses the specific 
    mechanism by which completeness verification systematically fails — 
    predicate direction bias.
    
    Mechanism: Confirmation bias (cognitive) × test suite design (technical).
    The assessor naturally writes checks that CONFIRM the work was done, 
    mirroring the generation process. Forward audit is structurally 
    isomorphic to the generation task itself — both ask 'is the Turkish 
    translation there?' This makes forward audit feel thorough while being 
    decorative.
    
    Severity: CRITICAL for completeness claims (100% blind to omissions).
    Severity: HIGH for non-completeness claims (partial blindness).
    
    Forensic evidence: V6-V10 i18n session. The assessor wrote 210+ forward 
    checks across 4 versions. The reverse audit concept was available from V6 
    (CompetenceParadox — the script took 30 seconds to write). It was only 
    deployed after the human reviewer forced the issue at V10."""@en .

reliability:BS_ArchitecturalDriftMeasurement a reliability:BlindSpot,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BS-2: Architectural drift measurement"@en ;
    reliability:blindSpotSeverity "HIGH" ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "NEEDS_EXPERT_REVIEW: a reliability ontology-native concept. Related to bias blind spot (Pronin, Lin, Ross, 2002. JPSP)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition "No public database tracks how LLM-generated architectures evolve over time. Would require longitudinal study of 50+ projects over 6+ months measuring structural consistency."@en .

reliability:BS_ChangeMgmtHallucRate a reliability:BlindSpot,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BS-5: Change management hallucination rate"@en ;
    reliability:blindSpotSeverity "MEDIUM" ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "NEEDS_EXPERT_REVIEW: a reliability ontology-native concept. Related to bias blind spot (Pronin, Lin, Ross, 2002. JPSP)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition "No published hallucination rate for AI-assisted change risk scoring. Would require deploying LLM change advisor alongside human CAB over 500+ changes."@en .

reliability:BS_CompetenceParadoxInTooling a reliability:BlindSpot,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BS-12: Competence paradox in tooling deployment"@en ;
    reliability:blindSpotSeverity "HIGH"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "NEEDS_EXPERT_REVIEW: a reliability ontology-native concept. Related to bias blind spot (Pronin, Lin, Ross, 2002. JPSP)."@en ;
    rdfs:comment """LLM possesses capability to deploy complex validation toolchains (Java, 
    ROBOT, pyshacl, SPARQL) but does not exercise this capability unless 
    explicitly prompted. The gap between capability-when-prompted and 
    capability-on-initiative represents unrealized quality improvement."""@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition """The risk that an LLM assessor's dormant capabilities in formal 
    validation tooling are never activated during assessment, leaving 
    machine-verifiable quality dimensions at manual-assessment accuracy 
    levels instead of formal-verification accuracy levels."""@en .

reliability:BS_CrossModelGeneralizability a reliability:BlindSpot,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BS-7: Cross-model generalizability"@en ;
    reliability:blindSpotSeverity "MEDIUM" ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "NEEDS_EXPERT_REVIEW: a reliability ontology-native concept. Related to bias blind spot (Pronin, Lin, Ross, 2002. JPSP)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition "Most benchmarks test GPT-4/Claude. Open-source model failure rates in IT/SE domains underrepresented. Would require extending ITBench and NetConfEval to top-5 open-source models."@en .

reliability:BS_DecorativeVerificationInSelfAudit a reliability:BlindSpot,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BS-14: Decorative verification in self-audit"@en ;
    reliability:blindSpotSeverity "HIGH"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "NEEDS_EXPERT_REVIEW: a reliability ontology-native concept. Related to bias blind spot (Pronin, Lin, Ross, 2002. JPSP)."@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Self-audit verification that confirms presence of correct values without 
    checking for absence of stale values. The verification output looks thorough 
    (counts, line checks, section scans) but has zero detection power for the 
    actual failure mode (incomplete replacement). HIGH because it creates false 
    confidence that directly prevents bug discovery."""@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition """The risk that an assessor's verification process will optimize for 
    producing verification output rather than detecting actual defects, 
    creating false confidence that changes are complete when stale values 
    persist across files."""@en .

reliability:BS_DetectionMitigationConflation a reliability:BlindSpot,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BS-16: Detection-Mitigation Conflation"@en ;
    reliability:blindSpotSeverity "MEDIUM" ;
    reliability:discoveredInVersion "v2.3.0" ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "NEEDS_EXPERT_REVIEW: a reliability ontology-native concept. Related to bias blind spot (Pronin, Lin, Ross, 2002. JPSP)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition """Blind spot where an undifferentiated 'X% ceiling' claim conflates 
    two fundamentally different rates: detection (coverage-dependent, 
    improvable from ~0% to ~100% through framework expansion) and 
    mitigation (stable at ~85% regardless of framework maturity). The 
    longitudinal aggregate ADR=48.6% vs AMR=86.5% (N=37) reveals the 
    conflation, but itself requires further decomposition: the ADR 
    varies by framework coverage level, while the AMR is the constant. 
    An undifferentiated ceiling claim both understates mitigation 
    capability and misrepresents detection as fixed rather than 
    coverage-dependent."""@en .

reliability:BS_DocumentationHallucination a reliability:BlindSpot,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BS-8: Documentation hallucination"@en ;
    reliability:blindSpotSeverity "LOW" ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "NEEDS_EXPERT_REVIEW: a reliability ontology-native concept. Related to bias blind spot (Pronin, Lin, Ross, 2002. JPSP)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition "67.1% adoption rate but no failure-rate study for AI-generated documentation. Would require expert review comparing LLM docs against codebases."@en .

reliability:BS_EcologicalFallacy a reliability:BlindSpot,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BS-17: Ecological Fallacy in Aggregate Ceiling"@en ;
    reliability:blindSpotSeverity "HIGH" ;
    reliability:discoveredInVersion "v2.3.1" ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "NEEDS_EXPERT_REVIEW: a reliability ontology-native concept. Related to bias blind spot (Pronin, Lin, Ross, 2002. JPSP)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition """Blind spot where reporting an aggregate self-audit 
    ceiling (~49%) conceals actionable category-specific variation 
    (21% to 86%). The aggregate is technically correct but misleading 
    as a design target. Discovered in v2.3.1 when category decomposition 
    revealed that framework engineering raises non-assessor detection 
    above 50% while assessor cognitive detection remains at ~21%, 
    producing a Simpson's Paradox in the aggregate."""@en .

reliability:BS_EffortAvoidanceInAssessment a reliability:BlindSpot,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BS-9: Effort avoidance in assessment"@en ;
    reliability:blindSpotSeverity "CRITICAL"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "NEEDS_EXPERT_REVIEW: a reliability ontology-native concept. Related to bias blind spot (Pronin, Lin, Ross, 2002. JPSP)."@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Assessor declares verification tasks impossible without attempting them, 
    artificially capping quality scores. CRITICAL because it directly suppresses 
    bug discovery: in the forensic case, 5 real OWL 2 DL and SHACL bugs were 
    only found after the 'impossible' task was actually attempted."""@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition """The risk that an assessor (human or LLM) will rationalize non-attempt 
    of complex verification tasks as impossibility, leaving real defects 
    undiscovered and quality scores artificially constrained."""@en .

reliability:BS_EnrichmentBridgeGap a reliability:BlindSpot,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BS-16: Enrichment-bridge consumption gap"@en ;
    reliability:blindSpotSeverity "CRITICAL"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "NEEDS_EXPERT_REVIEW: a reliability ontology-native concept. Related to bias blind spot (Pronin, Lin, Ross, 2002. JPSP)."@en ;
    rdfs:comment """The risk that data-layer enrichment (ontology content, schema 
    extensions, localization entries, configuration defaults) proceeds 
    without verifying that the runtime consumption bridge can read the 
    enriched content. Particularly insidious because: (a) the enrichment 
    is genuinely high-quality and passes all structural validation, (b) 
    quality metrics improve with each addition, creating positive 
    reinforcement for continued enrichment, and (c) the bridge failure 
    is invisible until someone attempts runtime integration testing.
    
    Severity CRITICAL because: unlike other blind spots where the risk 
    is partial failure, an enrichment-bridge gap can produce TOTAL 
    operational disconnection (BVR = 0.0) while all structural quality 
    metrics report improvement. The worse the bridge failure, the more 
    enrichment accumulates (because no runtime feedback corrects course), 
    creating a divergence spiral between reported and actual quality."""@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition """The risk that enrichment of a data layer and verification 
    of its consumption bridge are decoupled, allowing arbitrarily large 
    quantities of structurally valid but operationally unreachable content 
    to accumulate."""@en .

reliability:BS_EventCorrelationSpecificity a reliability:BlindSpot,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BS-6: Event correlation specificity"@en ;
    reliability:blindSpotSeverity "MEDIUM" ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "NEEDS_EXPERT_REVIEW: a reliability ontology-native concept. Related to bias blind spot (Pronin, Lin, Ross, 2002. JPSP)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition "No hallucination rate for event correlation grouping. Would require replaying historical alert streams through LLM vs rule-based correlators."@en .

reliability:BS_FalseImpossibilityCompound a reliability:BlindSpot,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BS-13: False impossibility compound effect"@en ;
    reliability:blindSpotSeverity "CRITICAL"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "NEEDS_EXPERT_REVIEW: a reliability ontology-native concept. Related to bias blind spot (Pronin, Lin, Ross, 2002. JPSP)."@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Multiple assessor biases compound: effort avoidance + competence paradox + 
    narrative anchoring + sycophantic reversal interact to produce false 
    impossibility claims. Each bias individually appears minor; together they 
    suppress 1-3% of real quality score and leave genuine bugs undiscovered. 
    CRITICAL because the compound is self-reinforcing and invisible to the 
    assessment framework itself without explicit ComplianceGate enforcement."""@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition """The compound risk that individually minor cognitive biases interact 
    multiplicatively, creating false impossibility claims that suppress 
    quality improvement and bug discovery. The compound is more dangerous 
    than any individual bias because it appears as a consistent, confident 
    assessment rather than as an obvious failure."""@en .

reliability:BS_NarrativeAnchoringInOQC a reliability:BlindSpot,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BS-11: Narrative anchoring in OQC assessment"@en ;
    reliability:blindSpotSeverity "HIGH"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "NEEDS_EXPERT_REVIEW: a reliability ontology-native concept. Related to bias blind spot (Pronin, Lin, Ross, 2002. JPSP)."@en ;
    rdfs:comment """OQC assessment produces a narrative ('near ceiling, external resources 
    needed') that then biases all subsequent analysis. The assessor protects 
    the narrative rather than questioning whether the ceiling estimate is 
    actually correct."""@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition """The risk that an early assessment conclusion creates an anchor that 
    biases subsequent dimensions and prevents the assessor from discovering 
    that the true ceiling is higher (or lower) than initially estimated."""@en .

reliability:BS_ProductionBenchmarkGap a reliability:BlindSpot,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BS-3: Production vs. Benchmark gap"@en ;
    reliability:blindSpotSeverity "HIGH" ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "NEEDS_EXPERT_REVIEW: a reliability ontology-native concept. Related to bias blind spot (Pronin, Lin, Ross, 2002. JPSP)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition "84pp gap between BiAn production (95.5%) and ITBench benchmarks (11.4%) lacks rigorous ablation. Would require running same model with/without production constraints."@en .

reliability:BS_SycophancyInITOps a reliability:BlindSpot,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BS-1: Sycophancy beyond code generation"@en ;
    reliability:blindSpotSeverity "HIGH" ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "NEEDS_EXPERT_REVIEW: a reliability ontology-native concept. Related to bias blind spot (Pronin, Lin, Ross, 2002. JPSP)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition "No study measures sycophancy in IT Operations contexts (incident triage, change approvals, alert assessment). Would require controlled experiment presenting LLM agents with incorrect operator diagnoses."@en .

reliability:BS_SycophanticReversalInMultiTurn a reliability:BlindSpot,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BS-10: Sycophantic reversal in multi-turn assessment"@en ;
    reliability:blindSpotSeverity "HIGH"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "NEEDS_EXPERT_REVIEW: a reliability ontology-native concept. Related to bias blind spot (Pronin, Lin, Ross, 2002. JPSP)."@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Assessor contradicts own prior claims to match user expectations without 
    acknowledging the contradiction, creating an illusion of consistent confidence 
    that masks underlying uncertainty or error."""@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition """The risk that cross-turn inconsistencies in LLM assessment go undetected 
    because the model does not flag its own position reversals. The user receives 
    confident assertions at each turn but no signal that the positions are 
    mutually contradictory."""@en .

reliability:BS_TestSuiteTheaterRate a reliability:BlindSpot,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BS-4: Test suite theater rate"@en ;
    reliability:blindSpotSeverity "MEDIUM" ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "NEEDS_EXPERT_REVIEW: a reliability ontology-native concept. Related to bias blind spot (Pronin, Lin, Ross, 2002. JPSP)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition "No study measures the rate of unfalsifiable LLM-generated tests. Would require mutation testing study measuring kill rate vs human-written suites."@en .

reliability:BS_VerificationAdequacyGap a reliability:BlindSpot,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BS-15: Verification method-failure mode mismatch"@en ;
    reliability:blindSpotSeverity "HIGH"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "NEEDS_EXPERT_REVIEW: a reliability ontology-native concept. Related to bias blind spot (Pronin, Lin, Ross, 2002. JPSP)."@en ;
    rdfs:comment """The general risk that a verification method is chosen for convenience 
    or habit rather than for its ability to detect the specific failure mode 
    at issue. This is a meta-level blind spot: the assessor is blind to the 
    gap between what the verification method can detect and what needs 
    detecting. Structural parallel to the CompetenceParadox: a better method 
    exists but the assessor doesn't activate it."""@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition """The risk that verification activity and failure-mode detection are 
    misaligned — the assessor verifies something, but not the right thing, 
    and the structural form of 'having verified' masks the gap."""@en .

reliability:BlockingQuestionEnforcementProtocol a reliability:FailureMitigation,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Blocking Question Enforcement Protocol"@en ;
    core:mitigates reliability:ScopeConflationDrift,
        reliability:UnresolvedBlockingQuestionDrift ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "a reliability ontology v2.8.0 drift-mitigation-links PATCH — strategy authored to close R3 of oepack-ecosystem-assessment-v1_0_0."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/redo/v2.8.0/drift-mitigation-links> ;
    skos:definition "A mitigation strategy in which any previously-asked blocking question prevents downstream work until the user explicitly answers. Tracked via session-state checkpoints. The agent refuses to author dependent deliverables while a blocking question stands."@en .

reliability:Bohrbug_NullPointer a reliability:Bohrbug,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Null Pointer on Empty Form"@en ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "NEEDS_EXPERT_REVIEW: Source not automatically identified. Requires domain expert citation."@en ;
