    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition """Version progression for non-assessor categories: 
    v1.2: 0/3=0%, v2.0.4: 2/7=29%, v2.0.6: 2/10=20%, 
    v2.0.7: 12/12=100% (CI:[73.5%,100%]), 
    v2.1.0: 0/3=0% (new assessor category discovered), 
    v2.2.0: 2/2=100% (assessor category formalized). 
    Pattern: step-function improvement within known categories, 
    reset to ~0% when new category emerges."""@en .

reliability:EmpiricalAsymmetryEvidence a reliability:DetectionMitigationAsymmetry,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Empirical Asymmetry Evidence (N=37)"@en ;
    reliability:autonomousDetectionRate 0.486 ;
    reliability:autonomousMitigationRate 0.865 ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    dcterms:source "a reliability ontology development sessions, February 2026. 8+ sessions, 37 classified failure instances."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/reliability> ;
    skos:definition """Longitudinal empirical evidence from a reliability ontology development 
    lifecycle (v1.2 through v2.2.0). Classification: Detection -- 
    Autonomous 18/37 (48.6%), Human-Prompted 8/37 (21.6%), 
    Human-Detected 11/37 (29.7%). Mitigation -- Autonomous 32/37 (86.5%), 
    Human-Guided 5/37 (13.5%). Category breakdown: Knowledge failures 
    (ADR=67%, AMR=89%), Structural failures (ADR=86%, AMR=71%), 
    Behavioral failures (ADR=43%, AMR=71%), Assessor cognitive failures 
    (ADR=21%, AMR=100%). The assessor category has the LOWEST autonomous 
    detection (21%) but HIGHEST autonomous mitigation (100%), confirming 
    the asymmetry is strongest for self-assessment failures.
    
    IMPORTANT: These are longitudinal aggregates spanning immature to 
    mature a reliability ontology versions. The ADR=48.6% mixes early sessions (v1.2, 
    ~0% detection) with late sessions (v2.0.7+, ~100% for formalized 
    categories). Per FrameworkMaturationEffect: as a reliability ontology coverage 
    expands, the effective ADR for subsequent sessions increases. The 
    AMR=86.5% is the stable constant across all maturity levels."""@en .

reliability:EmpiricalCategorySpecificEvidence a reliability:CategorySpecificCeiling,
